WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.502 --> 00:00:03.915
- So I'm asked one question most often.

00:00:03.915 --> 00:00:06.383
Why didn't you sell your business?

00:00:06.383 --> 00:00:08.043
It doesn't even make money.

00:00:08.043 --> 00:00:09.876
By deleting images after you sent them,

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:12.466
we changed what it
meant to take a picture.

00:00:12.466 --> 00:00:14.768
I think one of the perks of
being a really small company

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:17.633
is that from the beginning, we
got to think about diversity.

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.553
- He's an American internet entrepreneur.

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:22.955
He's the co-founder and CEO
of the mobile app, Snapchat.

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:26.555
He has an estimated net worth
of $2.1 billion dollars.

00:00:26.555 --> 00:00:27.774
He's Evan Spiegel,

00:00:27.774 --> 00:00:30.688
and here's my take on his
top ten rules for success.

00:00:30.688 --> 00:00:32.145
Rule number five is my personal favorite

00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:33.260
and I'm curious to figure out

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.618
which one you guys like the best.

00:00:34.618 --> 00:00:36.064
As always, if Evan is saying something

00:00:36.064 --> 00:00:37.584
that really resonates with you,

00:00:37.584 --> 00:00:39.158
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:39.158 --> 00:00:43.268
and put quotes around it so
others can be inspired as well.

00:00:43.268 --> 00:00:45.698
(airplane flying)

00:00:45.698 --> 00:00:48.562
- So I'm asked one question most often.

00:00:48.562 --> 00:00:51.488
Why didn't you sell your business?

00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:53.641
It doesn't even make money.

00:00:53.641 --> 00:00:54.802
It's a fad.

00:00:54.802 --> 00:00:56.619
You could be on a boat right now.

00:00:56.619 --> 00:00:58.192
Everybody loves boats.

00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:00.109
What is wrong with you?

00:01:01.204 --> 00:01:02.811
(laughs)

00:01:02.811 --> 00:01:04.687
And I'm now convinced that the fastest way

00:01:04.687 --> 00:01:06.092
to figure out if you're doing something

00:01:06.092 --> 00:01:07.893
that is truly important to you,

00:01:07.893 --> 00:01:11.346
is to find someone who will
offer you a bunch of money

00:01:11.346 --> 00:01:13.364
to part with it.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:15.178
See the best thing is that
no matter whether or not

00:01:15.178 --> 00:01:17.715
you sell, you will learn
something very valuable

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:19.148
about yourself.

00:01:19.148 --> 00:01:20.889
If you sell, you will know immediately

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:23.159
that it wasn't the right dream anyways.

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:25.429
And if you don't sell, you're
probably on to something.

00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:27.935
Maybe you have the beginning
of something meaningful.

00:01:27.935 --> 00:01:30.420
- Do you worry about making the leap from

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.587
an inventor to a manager and
how are you handling that?

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.691
- Yeah, well I think that the solution

00:01:38.691 --> 00:01:40.413
really is the team, right?

00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:42.771
And so Bobby and I both have leaned

00:01:42.771 --> 00:01:45.057
really heavily on some
really terrific people

00:01:45.057 --> 00:01:47.421
that we've been able to hire

00:01:47.421 --> 00:01:51.334
and frankly, I think that's
the only way to do it.

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:52.902
I think that the
challenging part from like

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:55.437
the learning perspective
is making commitments

00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:57.956
that are that big at such a young age

00:01:57.956 --> 00:02:00.564
to the people that work
for us and their families.

00:02:00.564 --> 00:02:02.617
It's very hard for me to
use my phone to be honest.

00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:04.236
I'm kind of like on text message and email

00:02:04.236 --> 00:02:07.347
and our service so I'm
always yelling at our people

00:02:07.347 --> 00:02:09.149
to try and, hopefully not yelling,

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:11.262
but trying to encouraging,

00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:14.179
vigorously, vigorous encouragement,

00:02:15.475 --> 00:02:18.298
to make this service more and more simple.

00:02:18.298 --> 00:02:20.597
So every time we add a new feature,

00:02:20.597 --> 00:02:22.513
we try to take a button away.

00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:25.676
That sort of has spawned
a lot of our inventions,

00:02:25.676 --> 00:02:28.850
like we have one camera
button on our service

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.552
and you can tap it to take a photo

00:02:30.552 --> 00:02:31.920
but if you want to take a video,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.994
you just press and hold.

00:02:32.994 --> 00:02:34.802
And that in the past, if you remember,

00:02:34.802 --> 00:02:36.770
still happens on a lot
of camera phones today,

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.562
you have to switch into video mode,

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:40.705
and you end up with all
these like weird videos

00:02:40.705 --> 00:02:41.663
that you thought were pictures,

00:02:41.663 --> 00:02:42.958
and pictures that you thought were videos,

00:02:42.958 --> 00:02:44.391
doesn't really make sense.

00:02:44.391 --> 00:02:48.878
So we're much more interested
in increasing engagement

00:02:48.878 --> 00:02:50.878
rather than getting big.

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:53.877
So we really focus on a few markets

00:02:53.877 --> 00:02:55.885
that are really important
to us and right now,

00:02:55.885 --> 00:02:57.586
we're working on really
increasing engagement

00:02:57.586 --> 00:02:59.402
and making sure that the
people that are on our service

00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:01.063
just love it.

00:03:01.063 --> 00:03:05.005
And that's what we watch
and I think over time,

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:06.996
we'll think about expanding the product

00:03:06.996 --> 00:03:09.653
but right now, it's really
just a maniacal focus

00:03:09.653 --> 00:03:12.574
on making great products
and deepening the engagement

00:03:12.574 --> 00:03:14.130
with our service.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.627
You already have inside of
you all of the amazing things

00:03:16.627 --> 00:03:19.424
you need to follow the
dreams that you have.

00:03:19.424 --> 00:03:21.850
And if you get stuck along
the way, there is a ton

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.138
of free information
available on the internet.

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:27.325
Have faith in yourself

00:03:27.325 --> 00:03:28.759
and the person you're going to become.

00:03:28.759 --> 00:03:31.157
Know that you are capable
of all of the growth

00:03:31.157 --> 00:03:32.869
that will be expected of you

00:03:32.869 --> 00:03:35.550
and that you expect from yourself.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.146
You will tackle every
challenge headed your way

00:03:38.146 --> 00:03:42.151
and if you don't, it won't
be for lack of trying.

00:03:42.151 --> 00:03:45.564
Someone will always have
an opinion about you.

00:03:45.564 --> 00:03:47.799
Whatever you do won't ever be enough

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:49.639
so find something important to you.

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:51.652
Find something that you love.

00:03:51.652 --> 00:03:54.625
We really found something
special in Snapchat,

00:03:54.625 --> 00:03:56.628
which at the time was called Picaboo,

00:03:56.628 --> 00:03:59.891
and we found that by deleting
the images after you sent them

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:02.811
we changed what it
meant to take a picture.

00:04:02.811 --> 00:04:05.526
So a picture wasn't about saving
a really important moment,

00:04:05.526 --> 00:04:07.438
it was about communicating.

00:04:07.438 --> 00:04:09.931
And we got really lucky
because smart phones

00:04:09.931 --> 00:04:11.867
and their camera connected to the internet

00:04:11.867 --> 00:04:15.013
meant that there really
was a big change happening

00:04:15.013 --> 00:04:17.873
in communication that we
didn't totally identify

00:04:17.873 --> 00:04:19.017
at the time but we do now,

00:04:19.017 --> 00:04:21.468
which is that people prefer to communicate

00:04:21.468 --> 00:04:24.052
with photos and videos rather than text.

00:04:24.052 --> 00:04:28.219
Diversity for us is really
closely tied to competency.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.441
So for us we have such a
diverse group of people

00:04:31.441 --> 00:04:33.758
using our products and services everyday,

00:04:33.758 --> 00:04:36.161
that in order for us to
make absolutely great

00:04:36.161 --> 00:04:38.587
products and services for that community,

00:04:38.587 --> 00:04:41.447
we need a really really
diverse group of people.

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.001
And it's really that simple.

00:04:44.001 --> 00:04:45.981
So for us, we just have an
awareness that you know,

00:04:45.981 --> 00:04:47.898
if we want to make the best products,

00:04:47.898 --> 00:04:50.400
we need to have a diverse group.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.664
- So can you describe your
diverse group of people,

00:04:52.664 --> 00:04:56.435
I mean, what are some of the percentages

00:04:56.435 --> 00:04:58.457
if you have them?

00:04:58.457 --> 00:05:00.286
- Again, this is sort of the challenge

00:05:00.286 --> 00:05:02.701
and I should have exact
percentages for you

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:04.524
but we just don't think about diversity

00:05:04.524 --> 00:05:06.236
in terms of numbers that way.

00:05:06.236 --> 00:05:08.808
And I think one of the perks
of being a really small company

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.275
is that from the beginning, we
got to think about diversity

00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:13.289
so we didn't end up with a situation

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:14.926
where 10 years down the line,

00:05:14.926 --> 00:05:17.112
oh my gosh I need to fix my numbers.

00:05:17.112 --> 00:05:19.031
Because it's not really
cool to think about people

00:05:19.031 --> 00:05:20.114
as numbers, right?

00:05:20.114 --> 00:05:22.351
We think about people
and diverse skill sets

00:05:22.351 --> 00:05:25.097
and so, for us, you know,
we're 300 people now

00:05:25.097 --> 00:05:27.975
we were 30 people gosh,
a year and a half ago,

00:05:27.975 --> 00:05:29.803
we've been really mindful that as we grow,

00:05:29.803 --> 00:05:31.999
we need to hire diverse folks.

00:05:31.999 --> 00:05:35.289
Conformity is so fascinating
and it's so pervasive

00:05:35.289 --> 00:05:38.139
that it's been studied
for a very long time.

00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:39.585
See it turns out, there are two things

00:05:39.585 --> 00:05:43.346
that can dramatically reduce
conformity in a group setting.

00:05:43.346 --> 00:05:47.126
The first is a single dissenting voice

00:05:47.126 --> 00:05:49.675
and the second is the ability
to communicate privately

00:05:49.675 --> 00:05:52.780
with other members of the group.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.618
Our government gives
us the right to privacy

00:05:55.618 --> 00:05:57.820
and the right to express ourselves freely

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.791
in the hope that we might
mitigate conformity.

00:06:00.791 --> 00:06:04.844
Democracy wasn't designed
to promote popular thought.

00:06:04.844 --> 00:06:07.920
It was architected to protect dissent.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.778
For as President Kennedy said,

00:06:09.778 --> 00:06:11.943
"Conformity is the jailer of freedom,

00:06:11.943 --> 00:06:13.894
"and the enemy of growth."

00:06:13.894 --> 00:06:15.727
- What circumstances were present

00:06:15.727 --> 00:06:18.832
that made this idea catch fire?

00:06:18.832 --> 00:06:21.208
- Sure, so I think there
were sort of two components

00:06:21.208 --> 00:06:24.035
and the first component is probably

00:06:24.035 --> 00:06:26.363
that social media was starting
to feel a little stale.

00:06:26.363 --> 00:06:29.100
So there's only so many
beautiful, attractive, cool

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.734
pictures that you can take

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:32.932
and when you see all of them
day after day after day,

00:06:32.932 --> 00:06:34.350
it just kind of gets boring.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.722
So I think there was that aspect of it

00:06:36.722 --> 00:06:39.592
and I think the other thing
is that we really identified

00:06:39.592 --> 00:06:44.108
that this ephemeral thing
was core to conversation.

00:06:44.108 --> 00:06:45.745
And so if you wanted
to have a conversation,

00:06:45.745 --> 00:06:47.243
one of the best parts about it

00:06:47.243 --> 00:06:48.931
was that you were left with the memory,

00:06:48.931 --> 00:06:50.845
but not necessarily a transcript.

00:06:50.845 --> 00:06:55.012
And I think that that really
drove the great feeling

00:06:56.105 --> 00:06:58.195
that you have when you
share snaps with friends

00:06:58.195 --> 00:07:02.247
and they're silly and not exactly perfect.

00:07:02.247 --> 00:07:03.936
You know, there was obviously a place for

00:07:03.936 --> 00:07:06.086
all of these great photos
that you wanted to save

00:07:06.086 --> 00:07:07.659
and keep and there wasn't so much a place

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:09.238
to have a conversation.

00:07:09.238 --> 00:07:11.843
And I think there's a
nice balance with both

00:07:11.843 --> 00:07:14.091
and so I think we sort
of filled maybe a void,

00:07:14.091 --> 00:07:16.785
the conversation void that was missing.

00:07:16.785 --> 00:07:20.080
I'm obsessed with this
idea of no standard terms.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.326
So when we were first getting started

00:07:22.326 --> 00:07:26.264
and took financing, our
lawyers would take us through

00:07:26.264 --> 00:07:27.499
the documents and they'd say,

00:07:27.499 --> 00:07:29.741
oh don't worry about
it, it's all standard.

00:07:29.741 --> 00:07:31.980
Then at the time I was
like, oh sure, standard,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.494
that's great, no issues,
just standard, no problem.

00:07:35.494 --> 00:07:37.880
And I've since learned that standard means

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.241
either the person who's walking
you through the documents

00:07:41.241 --> 00:07:43.701
doesn't understand them or you could be

00:07:43.701 --> 00:07:45.971
getting taken advantage of.

00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:48.358
So when someone says something's standard,

00:07:48.358 --> 00:07:50.901
just ask why and why and why and why

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:53.210
until you really understand intricately

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.953
how the deal is structured.

00:07:54.953 --> 00:07:57.778
So I think that the big thing
that we took away from that

00:07:57.778 --> 00:08:00.612
is standard terms is a huge red flag,

00:08:00.612 --> 00:08:03.195
and beyond that just don't be afraid

00:08:03.195 --> 00:08:05.274
to continue asking why.

00:08:05.274 --> 00:08:06.763
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:06.763 --> 00:08:09.482
I made this video because
Prasad Marne or Mar-neh,

00:08:09.482 --> 00:08:11.686
I hope I got that okay, asked me to,

00:08:11.686 --> 00:08:13.198
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:08:13.198 --> 00:08:14.678
that you want me to profile next,

00:08:14.678 --> 00:08:16.401
leave it down in the comments below,

00:08:16.401 --> 00:08:17.614
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:17.614 --> 00:08:19.188
I'd also love to know
which of the 10 rules

00:08:19.188 --> 00:08:20.465
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:08:20.465 --> 00:08:21.972
What change you're going to make?

00:08:21.972 --> 00:08:24.872
What was the most touching
after watching this video?

00:08:24.872 --> 00:08:27.209
What are you going to apply
to your life or your business?

00:08:27.209 --> 00:08:29.410
Leave it down in the
comments and I will join

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:30.305
in the discussion.

00:08:30.305 --> 00:08:31.994
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:31.994 --> 00:08:34.659
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.058
and I'll see you soon.

00:08:36.058 --> 00:08:38.891
(airplane flying)

